  "text": "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b\\cdot 15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{90{a}^{4}{b}^{2}c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2}b)^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{a}^{2}|={a}^{2}$ and $|b|=b.$  Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}b\\sqrt{10c} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
